Braam is a small operating system in a browser tab.

There is no server behind it. The kernel, the shell and every program are
compiled to WebAssembly and run on this page.

Your files live in the browser's own storage and survive a reload. Only /tmp
is emptied when the system starts.

Every command in /bin is a real program in a worker of its own, so pipes,
redirection, background jobs and ^C all work:

    ls /bin | grep ed
    echo hello > notes
    cat notes

edit is a full-screen editor: type to insert, ^S saves, ^Q quits.
less pages through a long file, and q quits it.

Nothing is sent anywhere unless you ask for it. Two commands do:

    curl https://example.com          fetch a URL, like the real thing
    chat wss://some.server/room nick  talk to whoever else is in the room

Files move in and out through the browser, since the page cannot reach your
disk on its own:

    fimport                pick files; they land in /import
    fexport notes          download notes out of the browser

help pages every command, and q quits it. uname -a says what this is and what
it is running on, and df shows how much room the browser gave it. ps lists
what is running, and which of those are processes of their own.

/proc is the system describing itself as plain text — try cat /proc/host.
